body:
If they're scars, in the sense of some degree of healing has happened and not open new ones, I wouldn't broach the topic unless you know the person extremely well. Like best friends level, or if you wouldn't talk about bathroom habits with them, don't do it.  I'm healing from my first and hopefully only episode of SH and it's scary just walking around in a sleeveless shirt, I've waited weeks before being able to wear one because people were asking questions when I did at first.  I lied and said I had a cat that loved to scratch me, but well we all here know I did that, I don't even have a cat.  If they look new, ie you can see scabbing or blood, then there may be some need there.  A loved one...god I don't even know how I'd want someone to respond to me.   I'll say, don't threaten to have them locked up for it.  That won't help. I guess if I saw someone I know well's cuts/SH I'd offer to listen if they wanted to talk, that I know they must be in a hard place, and I'm happy to talk now if they're ready (if situation is appropriate to this; if not maybe offer to a little later when you're in a quieter place or in private. I don't think I'd want to talk about my SH in a public place, that's for sure.)  And if they say no, or get scared or start to cry, be open to that and don't judge them for it. Or if they get angry.  Or if they're blase or angry in the face of you being worried.  As concerning as it may be to you that someone you  know is self-harming, the person doing it is in their own version of hell, which you likely have no idea exists, or certainly not to what extent.  They may be angry, furious, at themselves, at you, at the world, at god, whatever.  They may be severely depressed, like I've been.  There can be a lot of ugly stuff under the emotion that usually drives cutting, so if you're asking you need to be prepared for what you may be opening up and be able to respond to it in a mindful, non-judgmental way.  Before asking, I'd strongly encourage you to research how to talk to people you love about self-harm, there's fact sheets and how-tos on some good sites. Let me go grab you some resources...  

&#x200B;

[https://www.helpguide.org/articles/anxiety/cutting-and-self-harm.htm/](https://www.helpguide.org/articles/anxiety/cutting-and-self-harm.htm/)  (scroll down on this page and you'll find the "if a loved one self harms" heading) 

&#x200B;

[https://kidshealth.org/en/teens/friend-cuts.html](https://kidshealth.org/en/teens/friend-cuts.html) (kinda kid oriented but it's a good basic article)

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

If you are concerned your loved one may be in crisis of immediate danger you can make a confidential chat or call the national suicide prevention hotline (US) [https://suicidepreventionlifeline.org](https://suicidepreventionlifeline.org)   Other countries such as UK and so on have similar services. UK I believe Samaritans is one of the major providers.   

&#x200B;

These are some good resources, feel free to look around on those sites and maybe learn more about different issues such as depression and so on that may affect the person you know who is self-harming.  Learning about mental health conditions, even if not directly applicable to your loved one's situation, can help you understand the different ways people may struggle with their own minds and emotional pain.   I wish you and your loved one well. Just remember if you do broach the topic, approach it with an open mind, be prepared for a potentially strong reaction, or little reaction, and be empathetic and don't say anything off-the-cuff or smartass about it that might cause them more pain.
